{-
   What a structural class refuses, and how it says so.

   `Structural.yana` is the accepting half and used to run in both stages; this one named a resolver
   because the two worded the refusal differently, and the difference was the point. The old
   shipping stage reported a missing instance - "no instance of TrivialCopy for (Guard)" - which is
   what selection said when the table had nothing. This stage answers *before* the table and
   therefore knows the difference between "nothing declares it" and "the compiler has already
   decided otherwise", so it says the second: a classification the compiler makes from the type is
   not one a program can fix by writing an instance.

   Writing the instance the message declines to suggest is refused where instances are declared, by
   `isStructuralClass`'s third reader - and it cannot be spelled here anyway, since a class with no
   members has no body to indent under the `:` the parser wants.
-}

data Guard {id: Int}

instance Drop(Guard):
    fn drop(->value: Guard) -> {} = {}

data Holder {guard: Guard}

fn (TrivialCopy(a)) copyable(x: a) -> Int = 1

-- A type with a teardown of its own.
fn direct() -> Int = copyable(Guard {id: 1})

-- And a record that holds one, which is the fold rather than a second rule.
fn held() -> Int = copyable(Holder {guard: Guard {id: 2}})

fn main() -> Int = direct() + held()
